# Feature Architecture

Five invariants: group by feature (bounded context); a feature never imports another feature —
interaction is page composition, ports, shared entities, or events only; dependencies point one
direction with no cycles; business rules live in a pure, framework-free core (`domain/` +
`application/`); the core declares ports and adapters implement them. Breaking one needs an ADR in
`docs/adr/`, not a quiet exception.

## Layers

A layer may only import from layers strictly below it.

| Layer | Owns |
|-------|------|
| `app/` | Providers, DI container, router assembly, bootstrap, root error/suspense boundaries |
| `pages/` | Route-level composition. Thin, lazy, no business logic and no workflows |
| `features/` | Business capabilities — the bulk of the code. Each is a pure core plus adapters |
| `entities/` | Pure models shared by 2+ features (`User`, `Money`) and event contracts — no transport |
| `shared/` | Generic, business-agnostic building blocks: UI kit, HTTP client, hooks, config |

A model used by one feature stays in that feature's `domain/`; promote to `entities/` on the second
real consumer. A rule spanning several models belongs to the feature that owns the capability.

## Feature segments

`domain/` models, value objects, policies — pure · `application/` use cases + port interfaces —
pure · `api/` transport, DTOs, mappers, query keys, port implementations · `state/` client-only
state · `hooks/` orchestration · `ui/` rendering · `utils/` formatting · `config/` constants ·
`index.ts` React public API · `domain.ts` pure public API.

Start minimal — `api/ + ui/ + index.ts`. Segments are earned, not scaffolded. Add `application/`
only when logic spans contexts, needs compensation, or must run outside React; reads stay in
react-query.

## Dependency direction

```
app/ → pages/ → features/* (via index.ts) → entities/* → shared/*
ui → hooks → application → domain      hooks ↘ api → domain      hooks ↘ state
pages/{route}/ui/ → features/*  (page-local composition wrapper)
app/container.ts → features/*/domain.ts  (binds a port, incl. a pure-rule port for another feature)
```

Forbidden, always: `domain/` or `application/` importing React, HTTP, react-query, or an adapter ·
`domain.ts → index.ts` · `state/ → api/` · `ui/ → api/` or `application/` · `shared/ → features/` ·
`features/ → app/` or `pages/` · **`features/a/* → features/b/*`, in any form — no `index.ts`, no
`domain.ts`, no deep path** · deep imports into another feature · any cycle in either graph.

A feature needing another feature's data, UI, or rule never imports it — see the
`feature-architecture` skill's `cross-feature.md` for the four sanctioned patterns (page
composition, port + adapter, shared entity, domain event).

## Non-negotiables

- Server state lives in the react-query cache. Never copy it into a store or `useState`.
- Filters, pagination, sort, selected id, and active tab live in the URL.
- DTOs never leave `api/` — map to domain types at the edge; ports speak domain types.
- Time, ids, randomness, and storage enter the core as ports, never as `Date.now()` in a rule.
- Expected business outcomes are return values; exceptions are for bugs.
- A multi-step command has one owning feature and an explicit compensation path.
- A feature invalidates only its own query keys; invalidators are plain functions, not hooks. A
  cross-feature cache reaction is an event subscription, never a call into another feature.
- Events are declared with `defineEvent` and published only from `application/` (via the
  `EventPublisher` port), `api/`, or `hooks/` — never `domain/`.
- No `export *` in an entry point, and no nested barrels.

For layout decisions, ports and use cases, state placement, cross-feature communication and
workflows, boundary enforcement, scaling, and the corner-case playbook, load the
`feature-architecture` skill.
